le 5 and '1' in Example 3, and I'm now verifying this against all edge cases and multi-block scenarios.\n\n**Swapping Halves Exactly**\n\nI've discovered that certain blocks precisely swap halves, maintaining their internal relative positions and row assignments. This phenomenon, clearly demonstrated by the '6' and '7' blocks in Example 2, suggests a direct mirroring of occupied coordinates across the central divider, not just a positional shift.\n\n**Applying Symmetry Rules**\n\nI'm now applying the newly discovered symmetry rules. Matched blocks, identified by identical row and relative column positions within their respective halves, remain static. All other blocks are then transferred to the opposing half, preserving their internal row and relative column configuration. I'm currently testing this logic against the provided test input.\n\n**Verifying Swapping Logic**\n\nI'm now cross-referencing the symmetry rules with the swapping mechanism. It appears that blocks identified as non-symmetrical across the divider not only transfer but also swap their relative positions within the opposing half if they don't occupy identical row/relative column slots. I'm currently validating this refined approach.\n\n**Checking Block Collisions**\n\nI'm now simulating the transfer of all non-symmetrical blocks. It appears that in the left half, the transferred blocks '7', '9', and '9' do not exhibit any row collisions. This confirms my current relocation logic holds for this scenario, and I'm proceeding to analyze the right half.\n\n**Simulating Block Transfers**\n\nI'm confirming that no row collisions occur when transferring the non-symmetrical blocks ('7', '9', '9') to the left half. They maintain their relative positions and do not overlap with existing blocks, validating the current logic for this scenario. Now I'm checking the right half.\n\n**Confirming Grid Swap**\n\nI've confirmed that when no blocks possess symmetrical positioning, the left and right halves of the grid 